WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.090
Avery now knows the issue is inconsistent instructions, not a team that refuses innovation.

2
00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:14.074
Before choosing a new process, Avery asks staff to walk through actual reservations and invites feedback from people who use the rooms.

3
00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:17.873
The goal is to understand friction before selecting a solution.

4
00:00:18.013 --> 00:00:23.948
Notice the sequence: define the problem, consult people, try a manageable pilot, then review.

5
00:00:24.088 --> 00:00:27.558
This is our teaching pathway, not a named theory from the chapter.

6
00:00:27.698 --> 00:00:31.246
Consultation happens while input can still change the plan.

7
00:00:31.386 --> 00:00:35.195
Presenting a finished decision and asking for comments is different.

8
00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:39.115
A pilot might test a revised confirmation message for one room.

9
00:00:39.255 --> 00:00:44.726
Avery would identify who prepares it, what training is needed, and how patrons can get help.

10
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:51.496
The team could check whether booking corrections decrease and whether staff can maintain the process within their available time.

11
00:00:51.636 --> 00:00:54.657
Participation does not give everyone a veto.

12
00:00:54.797 --> 00:00:58.834
Avery remains responsible for making and explaining decisions.

13
00:00:58.974 --> 00:01:02.415
Some suggestions will conflict, and resources are limited.

14
00:01:02.555 --> 00:01:05.257
Respectful disagreement can reveal a risk.

15
00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:11.719
A credible manager explains the tradeoff, follows through, and changes direction when the evidence calls for it.
